;   Our title screen.
;
;   Eurocom's source already loads a title scene: X7.PDS:997 says
;
;       titdat   load tit.dat       ;title scn chr+col data
;
;   and X0.PDS:624-625 hands it straight to the decompressor:
;
;       bnk 7,#btit               ;bank in title data
;       lda #<titdat              ;uncrunch title scn
;       ldx #>titdat
;       jsr unrunscn
;
;   So the screen is described entirely by the packed bytes at `titdat`, and by
;   nothing else. Eurocom's own 314-byte TIT.DAT is the starting point but not
;   the finished screen -- see src/magician/title/nametable.txt for what we
;   decoded out of it and what we changed.
;
;   This module overlays `titdat`. It does not change a line of vendor/, does not
;   add a call, and does not move a label: `dotitle` still reads `#<titdat`, and
;   the address that resolves to is the same one it always was. Everything that
;   makes the screen different is in the bytes we emit here.
;
;   The overlay has to be sized against its neighbour. X7.PDS:997-998 lays the
;   three scene blobs end to end --
;
;       titdat   load tit.dat
;       pwdat    load pw.dat
;       pandat   load pan.dat
;
;   -- so there are exactly 314 bytes between `titdat` and `pwdat` and not one
;   spare. A scene that packed one byte longer would put its tail on top of the
;   password screen's data, which fails as a wrong password screen rather than
;   as a failed build. asm/build.py measures the budget from these two symbols
;   and refuses to assemble a scene that does not fit, then reads the finished
;   image back and decodes it to confirm the ROM really does carry our screen.
;
;   Written by us. Eurocom's source is read-only and pinned; see README.md.

        org titdat              ; the label Eurocom's own `dotitle` already reads
titscn  incbin ..\..\asm\out\ours\TIT.DAT